upply chain  problem to woo samsung local and state  governments offered a slew of financial  incentives the city of taylor and  williamson county offered 90  property tax breaks for the first 10  years  the state of texas threw in 27 million  dollars of its own in return samsung  offered to make donations to local  schools and guaranteed career help for  taylor students  but some have questioned if subsidies  should even be necessary we are debating  legislation  to provide some  53  billion dollars  billion dollars  in corporate welfare with no strings  attached  to the highly profitable  microchip industry  samsung is one of the world's most  valuable companies it's worth around 388  billion dollars so we're a few miles  from downtown austin here downtown  austin is full of cranes big tech  companies coming that aren't getting  subsidies they're bringing jobs here and  they're not asking for anything in  return  why do you need subsidies in order to  build this plant and taylor if it's a  great business opportunity why not just  build it i think the incentive packages  certainly lure a lot of top companies  here it makes it a competitive advantage  not only country to country but just  within the states texas and williamson  county and the city aren't the only  areas that are offering incentive  packages certainly other places that we  look did the same  everybody would like to be able to get  these type of projects again in their  area because they can see the economic  development that will happen and that's  generational development too this is not  just short-term it's really a long-term  investment  samsung recently broke ground on the  taylor site and the company said its fab  would be up and running by the end of  2024.  james lewis is a technology and public  policy researcher at csis a dc think  tank the countries we have to worry  about are  china china basically china what happens  to  the american economy or to the american  consumer's ability to buy 